</div>

<p>The film’s writer-directorial trio of Jim Abrahams, David Zucker, and Jerry Zucker understood that viewers would come for the laughs, but as with&nbsp;<em>Zero Hour!&nbsp;</em>and the countless other disaster pictures at which <em>Airplane! </em>pokes fun, they’d&nbsp;stay to find out what happens. Hence the inspired choice to play the whole thing perfectly straight, no matter how groan-inducing the visual or verbal gags being delivered.</p>
<p>All this works only because Abrahams, Zucker, and Zucker cast the right actors: that is to say, non-funny ones. At the time of <em>Airplane!</em>‘s release, “most comedies relied on actors to be funny, but nobody had really tried making a comedy without comedians before.” This meant that “the comedy had to be there before the camera started rolling,” so that the actors being seemingly unaware of the humor <span style="font-size: 13pt;">would only make it funnier. </span>The personification of this method is, of course, Leslie Nielsen, who’d already put in a quarter-century of dramatic performances (including in no less a seventies disaster spectacle than <em>The Poseidon Adventure</em>), but whose deadpan turn as Dr. Rumack elevated him into a kind of superstardom of the absurd. Whether a parody like <em>Airplane! </em>would work if made today is difficult to say,&nbsp;<span style="font-size: 13pt;">but it nevertheless remains a lesson in storytelling for young filmmakers — comedic or otherwise.</span></p>
